In this lesson, we'll look at how to read a survey report without getting fooled by the numbers. Whenever you spot a poll in the news, your first job is to hunt down the basics, the who, what, when, where, and how many. Start by asking what population the claim is really about, like Australian adults, then check who was actually surveyed, for example a thousand online respondents. That number is the sample size, and bigger usually means less noisy. Next, ask how those people were chosen. Was it a proper random sample, a voluntary online poll, or just whoever was easy to grab? That matters a lot. Also note what was actually measured, whether it was a number like age, or a category like state. Then keep an eye out for red flags. A tiny sample claiming a precise national result is suspicious, self-selected online polls only capture motivated people, and leading questions can twist answers. If the report never explains how the sample was obtained, treat the numbers with caution. Now let's see it in action.